---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import BranchRivers from "../components/BranchRivers.astro";
import households from "../data/households.json";
import { people } from "../lib/people.js";

const entries = households.reduce((a, h) => a + h.members.length, 0);
const more = [
  ["/rule", "Under whose rule", "Spanish viceroys, Austrians, Bourbons, the French, then Italy — and why a Napoleonic filing rule is the reason this archive can go back past 1809 at all."],
  ["/arienzo", "Arienzo", "The home parish, and the Collegiate Church of Sant'Andrea, which holds baptisms from 1672."],
  ["/liguori", "The bishop next door", "From 1762 to 1775 the bishop of Sant'Agata de' Goti kept his second seat beside this church, and he was Alfonso Maria de' Liguori."],
  ["/valle-caudina", "The Valle Caudina", "Three villages four kilometres apart in two provinces, and the marriage pool that moved the family north."],
  ["/zampiello", "The Zampiello of Arpaia", "A surname with about 123 bearers in the world, four sisters who reached Queensland, and a war decoration found in the family's own papers."],
  ["/australia", "Brisbane", "Two brothers who married two sisters, the ship, and four Falco graves at Nudgee."],
  ["/name", "The name", "Falco was coined independently many times over. What that means for anyone searching it."],
  ["/places", "Places", "The six places that carry this family, and the four kilometres that crossed a border."],
];
---
<Base title="The Falco Archive" bare={true}>
  <figure class="fhero">
    <img src={u("/hero/arienzo-piazza.jpg")} alt="The piazza and collegiate church of a hill town in Campania" />
    <figcaption class="cap">
      <div class="kick">A family history, kept honestly</div>
      <h1>Nine generations, on one street in Campania.</h1>
      <p class="sub">Arienzo · Arpaia · Forchia · Benevento · Brisbane — written into the parish books
      of Sant'Andrea from 1767, and into the civil registers of four regimes after that.</p>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">This is an archive of <strong>the Falco of Arienzo</strong> — a parish, a street and
    the families on it — and of the one branch of them that reached Brisbane. It is not a pedigree.
    Of the {people.length} people named here, {people.length - 8} are not in the direct line.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <em>Falco</em> is the Latin for falcon, and it became a surname twice over: as a Lombard personal
      name handed down as a patronymic, and as a nickname for the quick, the sharp-eyed, or the
      falconer. It was coined independently, many times, in many places.
      <strong>There is no single founding Falco family.</strong> The work here is to establish which
      Falco are actually connected, and to say plainly where the evidence stops.
    </blockquote>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">Several Falco, one town</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Where each of them came from, and what joins them</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.4rem">A shared surname in one small town is suggestive and
    nothing more. Two Falco households on the same street may be brothers, cousins, or unrelated men
    who happen to carry a common Campanian name. This archive joins two of them only when a record says
    so — and draws the rest apart, even where joining them would make the better story.</p>
    <BranchRivers />
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a href={u("/lines")}>The lines, in full →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The document at the centre of it</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">1 October 1834, five in the morning</h2>
    <div class="hero-plate" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <img src={u("/plates/pasquale-falco-death-1834.jpg")} alt="Arienzo civil death register 1834, act 69 — the death of Pasquale Falco" />
      <div>
        <p>For as long as this family has had a pedigree, the head of it was a guess. Then two
        neighbours from <strong>strada Camellara</strong> walked to the town hall at Arienzo and
        reported the death of the man next door.</p>
        <blockquote style="margin:.9rem 0">…è morto nella sua propria casa <strong>Pasquale Falco</strong>,
        d'anni settantaquattro, <em>marito di <strong>Chiara Rivetti</strong></em>, di professione
        contadino, <em>figlio di <strong>Matteo Falco, defunto</strong>… e di
        <strong>Francesca Crisci, defunta</strong></em>.</blockquote>
        <p>One act names his wife, both his parents, his trade and his street — and confirms, from the
        civil register, what a parish baptism of 1767 had said sixty-seven years earlier.
        <a href={u("/antenati")}>How it was found →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Four things this archive has established</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>A head to the line</h3>
        <p><strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>, whose children were
        baptised at Arienzo from 1767 — replacing an ancestor who was never there. Two of those
        children's death acts name them again — Rosa's in 1830, Pasquale's in 1834.
        <a href={u("/direct-line")}>The spine →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>An address</h3>
        <p><strong>Strada Camellara.</strong> Six Falco householders on one short street, all
        <em>contadini</em> or <em>coloni</em>, and the neighbours who reported each death lived a few doors along.
        <a href={u("/lines")}>The street →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>{people.length} people, not eight</h3>
        <p>{households.length} households and {entries} register entries, most of them belonging to
        somebody who never continued the line. <a href={u("/wider-family")}>The wider family →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>One kinship, proved</h3>
        <p>In 1831 <strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong> declared a neighbour's death as <em>parente</em> — a
        kinsman — joining the line to a second Camellara household. Three others remain unjoined.
        <a href={u("/lines")}>What joins them →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Elsewhere in the archive</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      {more.map(([href, t, d]) => (
        <a class="cardlink" href={u(href)}>
          <div class="nm">{t}</div>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.4rem">{d}</p>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What is still missing</h2>
    <p class="dek">Eleven questions this archive cannot answer, and five doors that need a person
    rather than a machine — the unread Brisbane immigration files, the restricted Caserta scans, two
    volumes at Benevento that were never digitised, the marriage supplements, and the DNA matches.
    All of it, with the searches that have already failed, is set out at
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions</a>.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Nothing here is taken on trust because it appears in a
    family tree. Where a record has been seen, it is cited; where it has not, the claim is marked.
    See <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
